  "verbalization": "Fluphenazine is linked to Abcb1 through a series of molecular and pharmacological relationships. The evidence suggests that Fluphenazine, as an antipsychotic agent, is included in the class of Phenothiazines, which includes Trifluoperazine and Promethazine. Both Trifluoperazine and Promethazine are known to bind to Abcb1, a transporter protein involved in drug resistance and cellular uptake. This indicates that Fluphenazine may share a common mechanism of action with these compounds, potentially through their interaction with Abcb1. The presence of a lowest common ancestor between Fluphenazine and these compounds further supports a shared pathway in their pharmacological effects. While the direct binding of Fluphenazine to Abcb1 is not explicitly stated, the evidence points to a broader pharmacological relationship that involves Abcb1 through the actions of related compounds in the Phenothiazine class. This suggests that Fluphenazine may influence Abcb1 through indirect mechanisms, such as modulating the activity of other compounds in the same class.",
